{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0084","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How many dyads participated in the reciprocal peer tutoring data collection, and what was their composition?","answer":"12 American English-speaking dyads participated: 6 friends and 6 strangers, 6 boys and 6 girls, with a mean age of 13 years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0084","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How many total sessions and minutes of data were collected across all dyads in this study?","answer":"A total of 60 sessions were collected, amounting to 5400 minutes of data, from 5 hourly sessions per dyad over 5 weeks."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0084","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What F1 score did the proposed model achieve for recognizing social norm violation?","answer":"The model achieved an F1 score of 0.705 using long-term temporal and contextual information."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0084","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What was the inter-rater reliability (Krippendorff's alpha) for annotating social norm violation in this dataset?","answer":"The inter-rater reliability for social norm violation annotation was 0.75."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0084","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What were the inter-rater reliability scores for eye gaze, smile count, smile duration, and head nod annotations?","answer":"IRR was 0.89 for eye gaze, 0.75 for smile count, 0.64 for smile duration, and 0.99 for head nod."}
